  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nAfter another relatively cool start to the day on Tuesday, a \nnoticeable change will occur as high pressure departs to the east \nand winds turn out of the south southwest. The breezes will carry a \nmore uncomfortable air mass back into the area. Dew points will \nsurge back into the 60s, possibly reaching near 70 over and near \nsoutheast IN. A cold front will approach from the north northwest on \nTuesday night, slowing as it becomes aligned more parallel to the \nflow aloft. At least scattered showers/thunderstorms are expected \nover the northwest portion of the forecast area as the front \napproaches, but will struggle to make it as far southeast as KCVG-\nKILN-KCMH. Should any storms erupt Tuesday evening, considerable  \ninstability (potentially CAPE of 2K+ J/kg) and dry air aloft could \nsupport some strong wind gusts. However, a lack of shear would \nprevent good organization, and the better chance for storms is later \nin the night when instability will be more meager.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nAn anomalously strong ridge will start out anchored over the \nsouthern Appalachians, while a positively tilted trough aligns from \nsouth central Canada through the Pacific Northwest and into northern \nCalifornia. Faster west southwest flow between these two features \nwill reside from the central/northern Rockies through the Great \nLakes region. At the surface, a cold front will be positioned from \nthe eastern Great Lakes region through the Ohio Valley and into the \nmiddle Mississippi Valley. The models are in good agreement through \nthe end of the work week, with the strong ridge only gradually \nweakening with time. Meanwhile, fast-moving short energy will \ncontinue to ride from west to east along the periphery of the ridge \naxis across the Great Lakes/Ohio Valley regions, allowing a \nbaroclinic zone to oscillate back and forth across the region. The \nmodel agreement breaks down more so this weekend and into early next \nweek, specifically associated with the speed at which a northwestern \nCONUS closed low opens up and ejects eastward. Rain chances will \ncontinue to threaten across the Ohio Valley, with the heat likely \nnot relenting until late this weekend into early next week. \n\n850 mb temperatures will peak at around 20-21C through Thursday. \nThis will allow for many locations to see highs in the lower 90s \neach day, while lows at night only cool off to the upper 60s to \nlower 70s. As a cold front approaches from the northwest, scattered \nconvection will threaten locations mainly north of I-70 from \nWednesday through Thursday. There will be a high PWAT air mass in \nplace, with LREF mean PWATs of 1.9-2.0 inches noted, along with the \nsurface boundary aligning more parallel with the flow aloft. As \nsuch, locally heavy rainfall will be a threat where convection does \noccur. High pressure will temporarily move in from the north on \nFriday. Rain chances return this weekend and into early next week, \nbut confidence remains low on timing of the associated frontal \nsystems, given the model disagreement on the aforementioned ejecting \nupstream trough. Temperatures will continue to run close to 10 \ndegrees above normal each day through Saturday, with highs ranging \nfrom the mid 80s north to near 90 degrees south. Temperatures will \nthen be cooler Sunday and especially Monday.",
